1. Variables & Outputs
    - Terraform uses input variables to make configurations reusable and outputs to expose useful information after infrastructure is created.
    
2. Input Variables
    - Variables allow you to parameterize your Terraform configuration instead of hardcoding values.
    - Basic Variable Definition
        variables.tf
        variable "region" {
            type        = string
            description = "AWS region to deploy to"
            default     = "us-east-1"
        }
    - Components
        variable → Declares a variable.
        region → Variable name.
        type → Data type constraint.
        description → Documentation.
        default → Optional default value.

3. Common Variable Types
    String
        variable "region" {
            type    = string
            default = "us-east-1"
        }
    Number
        variable "instance_count" {
            type    = number
            default = 2
        }
    List
        variable "allowed_ports" {
            type    = list(number)
            default = [80, 443]
        }
    Map
        variable "tags" {
            type = map(string)
            default = {
                Team = "platform"
            }
        }

4. Using Variables
    - Variables are referenced using the var object.
    provider "aws" {
        region = var.region
    }
    resource "aws_instance" "web" {
        count         = var.instance_count
        ami           = data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id
        instance_type = "t3.micro"
    }
    resource "aws_security_group" "web" {
        dynamic "ingress" {
            for_each = var.allowed_ports

            content {
            from_port = ingress.value
            to_port   = ingress.value
            protocol  = "tcp"
            }
        }
    }

5. Providing Variable Values
    - Terraform supports several ways to supply values.
    1. .tfvars File (Recommended)
        terraform.tfvars
            region         = "eu-west-1"
            instance_count = 3
        - Terraform automatically loads terraform.tfvars.
    2. Command-Line Variables
            terraform apply -var="region=eu-west-1"
        - Multiple variables:
            terraform apply \
                -var="region=eu-west-1" \
                -var="instance_count=3"
    3. Environment Variables
        export TF_VAR_region="eu-west-1"
        export TF_VAR_instance_count=3
    - Terraform automatically reads variables prefixed with TF_VAR_.

6. Variable Precedence
    - When the same variable is defined in multiple places, Terraform uses the highest-priority value:
    Priority (Highest → Lowest)	    Source
    1	                            -var command-line arguments
    2	                            *.auto.tfvars files
    3	                            terraform.tfvars
    4	                            Environment variables (TF_VAR_*)
    5	                            Variable default values

7. Output Values
    - Outputs expose values after Terraform applies infrastructure.
    outputs.tf
    output "instance_ip" {
        description = "Public IP of the web server"
        value       = aws_instance.web.public_ip
    }
    - After terraform apply:
    Outputs:
        instance_ip = "54.123.45.67"

8. Multiple Outputs
    output "bucket_name" {
        description = "Name of the S3 bucket"
        value       = aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.bucket
        sensitive   = false
    }

9. Sensitive Outputs
    - Use sensitive = true for secrets.
        output "database_password" {
            value     = aws_db_instance.db.password
            sensitive = true
        }
    - Terraform hides sensitive values in CLI output:
    database_password = <sensitive>

10. Viewing Outputs
    - Show All Outputs
        terraform output
    - Example:
        instance_ip = "54.123.45.67"
        bucket_name = "my-app-bucket"
    - Show Specific Output
        terraform output instance_ip
    - JSON Format
        terraform output -json
    - Useful for automation and CI/CD pipelines.

11. Complete Example
    variable "region" {
        type    = string
        default = "us-east-1"
    }
    provider "aws" {
        region = var.region
    }
    resource "aws_s3_bucket" "app" {
        bucket = "my-demo-bucket"
    }
    output "bucket_name" {
        value = aws_s3_bucket.app.bucket
    }
    - Run:
        terraform apply
    - Result:
        Outputs:
            bucket_name = "my-demo-bucket"

12. Key Takeaways
    - Variables make configurations reusable and configurable.
    - Access variables with:
        var.variable_name
    - Values can come from defaults, .tfvars files, environment variables, or command-line arguments.
    - Outputs expose useful information after deployment.
    - Access outputs with:
        terraform output
    - Use sensitive = true for secrets and credentials.